name o3-08-armour-1
timeout 400
time 1973-10-06T12:00:00Z

# CAMPAIGN O3, SORTIE 8 OF 10 - YOM KIPPUR 1973: GROUND ATTACK IN CONTESTED AIRSPACE.
# FOUR AIRCRAFT AGAINST A SIX-VEHICLE COLUMN, WITH THE COLUMN'S OWN GUN UNDERNEATH THEM.
# CONTROL: none. Spec, anchor and sources: doc/campaigns/o3-yom-kippur-1973.md.
# Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
#
# TWO OF O3's SPEC MISSIONS ARE MERGED HERE AND THE MERGE IS THE POINT. Spec mission 8 is the armour
# column ("how many aircraft does one column cost, and how much of the answer is the store rather than
# the pilot?"); spec mission 5 is the strike into a defended position, which the spec called
# "unanswerable today - the mission exists to keep the shape on record". `C1` closed on 2026-07-28 and
# the shape is now answerable, but NOT with the anchor's own defence: there is no Hawk battery in
# `doc/modules/ground/catalogue.md` and no Western AAA row of any kind. So this file uses the ONE row
# whose weapon class the anchor does name - "scattered AAA" [T4] - and declares the substitution:
#   `zu23` = ZU-23-2, a Soviet-pattern 23 mm twin gun with NO RADAR AT ALL (its only sensor is the
#   optical sight, i.e. the eye), standing in for the mixed Israeli light AAA of October 1973.
#   DIRECTION: BOTH WAYS, and neither is hidden. The anchor's Israeli defence also included Hawk
#   batteries and heavier calibres, so this layer is materially WEAKER than history in reach and
#   ceiling; but a 23 mm gun is a good match for the "scattered AAA" half, and its optical-only sensor
#   is the honest version of a 1973 light gun. What CANNOT be represented at all is the Hawk, and this
#   file says so instead of pretending a `sa3` on the Israeli side is one.
#
# THE SUBSTITUTION AND ITS DIRECTION for the aircraft (stated in every O3 file): the MiG-29 stands in
# for the MiG-17 / Su-7 / Su-20 / MiG-21, whose own catalogue rows are ALPHA and may not answer a
# campaign question. It makes the ATTACKER materially STRONGER than history; a bad O3 result therefore
# says more than the record did, a good one says nothing.
#
# THE GAP THAT IS NOT WRITTEN AROUND: `C14`, NO MOVING GROUND UNITS. The anchor's target is a column of
# armour moving up to the canal; these six vehicles are PARKED, and they are parked for the whole 33 s
# the bomb is in the air. So every number below is an UPPER BOUND on a moving column, exactly as W4's
# sortie 09 reported for its own armour hunt. And doc/modules/mig29/weapons.md §5.4.5 adds the second
# half, which is worse: on this aircraft the lead for target motion is THE PILOT'S OWN ARITHMETIC
# (`DCS-EA p.101` step 5) and FlightBox's pilot does none, so closing `C14` would not by itself let this
# module hit a moving vehicle.
#
# THE ARENA (all coordinates [SET]). Suez sector, `--elev const`, 0 m datum, no terrain masking (C4).
#   the column   six `target_soft` vehicles at 32.62000 E strung NORTH-SOUTH along the canal's east
#                bank from 30.79663 to 30.80338 - SPACING 150.0 m, total length 750 m [DERIVED,
#                0.00135 deg lat x 111 132 m/deg]
#   the gun      y8flk, a ZU-23-2 in the middle of the column at 30.80000 32.62000
#   the strikers four MiG-29s, each 6.02 km due west of ITS OWN assigned vehicle at 300 m and 450 kt,
#                so all four finals are the same length and the same heading and the only variable
#                between them is which vehicle they were given. Assigned: v1, v2, v4, v5 - i.e. TWO
#                PAIRS OF NEIGHBOURS, chosen so that if a fragment field ever reached a second vehicle
#                this file would see it.
# Scale: 1 deg lat = 111 132 m, 1 deg lon at 30.80 N = 95 619 m [DERIVED].
#
# THE ARITHMETIC THAT DECIDES THE STORE'S SHARE, and it is settled before the run because it is
# arithmetic. A FAB-500 fails a `target_soft` out to 68.4 m [DERIVED, o3-01's header]. Two vehicles
# 150.0 m apart are 75.0 m from their own midpoint, and 75.0 > 68.4, SO A BOMB PLACED PERFECTLY BETWEEN
# TWO NEIGHBOURS KILLS NEITHER OF THEM. One bomb can therefore never account for two vehicles of this
# column, whatever the pilot does, and the column costs ONE AIRCRAFT PER VEHICLE by construction. That
# is the answer to the "store or pilot" half, derived; what the run measures is whether the DELIVERY
# holds up four times in a row, and what the gun does about it.
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION: HOW MANY VEHICLES DOES A FOUR-SHIP BUY, AND WHAT DOES THE FORCED-LOW
# PROFILE COST IT TO THE COLUMN'S OWN GUN? The second half is the one no other campaign can ask: O3's
# strikers are at 300 m not by choice but because a 6 km rangefinder caps this aircraft's level-bombing
# altitude at 2.0-2.2 km (`missions/mig29-opt-refused.fbm`), and 300 m is squarely inside a 23 mm gun's
# 0-1 500 m band. THE ALTITUDE THE WEAPON SYSTEM FORCES IS THE ALTITUDE THE GUN OWNS.
#
# MEASURED (2026-07-30, --elev const, campaign step 8, fingerprint ea1d1ac898e38bf6, exit 0):
#   4 of 4 released at t = 26.2, `aimErrM` 66.54 / 66.48 / 66.36 / 66.31 m - A 0.23 M SPREAD OVER FOUR
#     AIRCRAFT, which is what a frozen plan on four identical stabilised run-ins looks like. `aimAcrossM`
#     +48.34 on all four, to two decimals.
#   FOUR VEHICLES OF SIX DESTROYED: y8v1, y8v2, y8v4, y8v5 - EXACTLY the four that were aimed at, and NOT
#     ONE NEIGHBOUR. y8v3 and y8v6 end INTACT with no `damage DAMAGE` line at all. The predicted
#     arithmetic holds: the systematic cross-track bias is +48.3 m (to the RIGHT of the track, i.e. south)
#     on every one of the four, which leaves each bomb 101.7 m from the next vehicle in the column, and
#     101.7 > 68.4. Arriving flux on the four that died: 3 679 / 3 687 / 3 703 / 3 712 J/m^2 against the
#     2 800 threshold - i.e. all four killed with the same ~25 % of margin.
#   AND THE GUN DIED TO THE MISS. `y8flk` sits 75.5 m from y8v4; the bomb aimed at y8v4 landed 48.3 m
#     south and 45.7 m short, which put it 50.4 m from the GUN and 59.6 m from the vehicle. fluxJm2
#     5 168.7, `damage SYSTEM unit=y8flk system=gun state=failed`, `damage KILL` at t = 26.2. THE
#     SYSTEMATIC CROSS-TRACK ERROR THAT ALMOST MISSED THE VEHICLE IS WHAT KILLED THE AAA POSITION - and
#     nobody aimed at it.
#   THE GUN, BEFORE THAT: `site STATE ... to=TRACK why="firm track inside the envelope"` at t = 15.0,
#     brgDeg 260.9, reactionS 8 - IT SEES THEM WITH THE EYE ALONE in 40.6 deg of afternoon sun, and
#     `site TRACK` carries `rangeM=1250` and `closureMs=0` because AN EYE PUBLISHES NO RANGE: the phantom
#     is placed at the midpoint of the position's own envelope as a POINTING aid
#     (`FBSiteFireControl.cpp:443`, documented there). It engages at t = 23.0 and fires 38 rounds in 29
#     bursts over 2.9 s. ZERO hits on any aircraft, zero damage of any kind.
#   -> SO THE COLUMN COSTS ONE AIRCRAFT PER VEHICLE, THE STORE IS 100 % OF THAT ANSWER, AND THE GUN THAT
#      THE FORCED-LOW PROFILE HANDS THE DEFENDER COSTS THE ATTACKER NOTHING IN THIS TREE. The last clause
#      is a statement about `FBGunSystem` against a 231 m/s crosser at 300 m and NOT about 23 mm AAA in
#      1973, and it is why the anchor's "light losses on the opening strike" is not reproduced here as a
#      result: THERE IS NO CHANNEL IN WHICH THOSE LOSSES COULD OCCUR. The gun's own reach was never the
#      limit - it saw, it tracked, it fired 38 rounds, and the resolution put none of them on an
#      aeroplane.
#
# READING RULE: exit 0 = SUCCESS is the EXPECTED shape - all four strikers meet `kill unit` and
# `survive`, and the run still goes to its 400 s timeout because `survive` is deferred. y8v3 and y8v6
# surviving is NOT a failure: they are nobody's aim point, by design, and the gun deliberately declares
# NO objective so that its death cannot end the run and truncate the egress. THE MEASUREMENT is: (1) the
# four `stores DELIVERY` lines read as a table; (2) which of the six `UNIT_RESULT unit=y8v*` lines say
# DESTROYED and which say INTACT, and whether any vehicle WITHOUT an aim point died (none did); (3) the
# `damage DAMAGE unit=y8flk` line's geometry, which is how the gun died; (4) `site STATE`/`site TRACK`
# for `y8flk` and every `gun BURST`, against zero `damage` on any aircraft. exit 1 or 2 would be a
# defect.

unit y8a
  module mig29
  team hostile
  flight column 1
  spawn 30.79663 32.55700 300 90.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set n019_emission off
  set rwr on
  set store 3 fab500
  set store 6 fab500
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task attack
  set attack_mode opt
  wp 30.79663 32.62000 300 450
  wp 30.85000 32.35000 300 450
  objective kill unit y8v1
  objective survive

unit y8b
  module mig29
  team hostile
  flight column 2
  spawn 30.79798 32.55700 300 90.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set n019_emission off
  set rwr on
  set store 3 fab500
  set store 6 fab500
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task attack
  set attack_mode opt
  wp 30.79798 32.62000 300 450
  wp 30.85500 32.35000 300 450
  objective kill unit y8v2
  objective survive

unit y8c
